WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.120
- [Instructor] Let's say that
we have the polynomial p of x.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:06.170
And when expressed in factored
form, it is x plus two

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.670
times two x minus three

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:11.210
times x minus four.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:13.470
What we're going to do in this
video is use our knowledge

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:16.330
of the roots of this polynomial
to think about intervals

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:20.320
where this polynomial would
be positive or negative.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.820
And the key realization
is, is that the sign

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.600
of a polynomial stays the same
between consecutive zeros.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.360
Let me just draw an arbitrary
graph of a polynomial here

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.140
to make you appreciate why that is true.

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:33.890
So x-axis, y-axis.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:38.020
And if I were to draw some
arbitrary polynomial like that,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.120
you can see that between consecutive zeros

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:40.990
the sign is the same.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:44.350
Between this zero and this zero,
the polynomial is positive.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.540
Between this zero and this zero,
the polynomial is negative.

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.650
And that's almost intuitively true.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.640
Because if the sign did not stay the same,

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:54.470
that means you would
have to cross the x-axis.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:55.890
So you would have a zero,

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.150
but we're saying between
consecutive zeros.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.690
So between this zero and this
zero, it is positive again.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.920
Then after that zero, it stays negative.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.240
Once again, the only way it
wouldn't stay negative is

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.670
if there were another zero.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.960
So now let's go back to this example here.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.380
And let me delete this
because this is not the graph

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.250
of p of x, which I have just written down.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:18.260
Let's first think about its zeros.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.470
So the zeros are the x-values

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:22.450
that would either make
x plus two equal zero,

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.370
two x minus three equal zero,
or x minus four equal zero.

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:26.630
So first, we can think about,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.890
well, what x-values would make x plus two,

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.320
x plus two equal to zero?

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.050
Well, that, of course, would
be x equals negative two.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.920
What x-values would make two
x minus three equal zero?

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.270
Two x minus three equal to zero,

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:43.270
add three to both sides,
you get two x equals three.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.570
Divide both sides by two,
you get x equals 3/2.

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:50.260
And then last but not least,
what x-values would make

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:53.320
x minus four equal to zero?
(distant siren wailing)

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:58.100
Add four to both sides,
you get x is equal to four.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.840
And so if we were to plot this,

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.860
it would look something like this.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.129
So this x equals negative two,

00:02:07.129 --> 00:02:09.270
(distant siren wailing)
x equals negative one.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.280
This is zero.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.080
This is one, two,

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.970
three, and four.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:20.340
And let me draw the y-axis here.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.990
So the y-axis would look
something like this, x and y.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:27.480
We have a zero at x equals negative two,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.150
so our graph will
intersect the x-axis there.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.160
We have a zero at x is equal
to 3/2, which is 1 1/2,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.330
which is right over there.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:38.500
And we have a zero at x equals hour,

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.230
which is right over there.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.930
And so we have several
candidate intervals.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:46.610
And actually, let me write
this down in a table.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.180
So the intervals

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:50.960
over which and this is really,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:54.610
be between consecutive zeros,

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.940
intervals to consider.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.320
So I'll draw a little table here.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:03.680
So you have x is less than negative two.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.160
That's one interval.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:09.340
X is less than, actually,
let me color-code this.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.890
So if I were to say the interval

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.020
for x is less than negative two,

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:16.640
so that's this yellow that I
draw at the extreme left there.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.940
We could have an interval where

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.380
x is between negative two and 3/2,

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.580
so negative two is less than
x, is less than positive 3/2.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:30.550
That would be this
interval right over here.

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:34.720
You have the interval, I'm
trying to use all my colors,

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.570
between 3/2 and four, this interval here.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:43.320
So that would be 3/2 is less
than x, is less than four.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.190
And then last but not least,

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.660
you have the interval where
x is greater than four,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:50.450
that interval right over there.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.870
So x is greater,

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:55.840
greater than four.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.810
Now, there's a couple of ways
of thinking about whether,

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:03.890
over that interval, our function
is positive or negative.

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.603
One method is to just evaluate our p,

00:04:07.558 --> 00:04:10.210
our function at a point in the interval.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.043
And if it's positive,

00:04:11.043 --> 00:04:12.820
well, that's means that that
whole interval is positive.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:13.653
It's negative,

00:04:13.653 --> 00:04:15.390
that means that that whole
interval is negative.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:17.760
And once again, it's intuitive because,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.350
if for whatever reason, it were to switch,

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:20.740
we would have another zero.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:22.470
I know I keep saying that.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.470
But another way to think about it is,

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:27.930
over that interval, what is
the behavior of x plus two,

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.970
two x minus three, and x minus four?

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.690
Think about whether they're
positive or negative,

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.990
and use our knowledge
of multiplying positives

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.450
and negatives together to
figure out whether we're dealing

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.210
with a positive or negative.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:41.180
So let's do it, we could do it both ways.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.370
So let's think of this as our sample x,

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.990
sample x-value.

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.040
And then let's see what
we can intuit about

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.520
or deduce about whether,
over that interval,

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.250
we are positive or negative.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:58.690
So for x is less than negative two,

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.210
maybe an easy one or
an obvious one to use,

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:04.230
it could be any value where
x is less than negative two,

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:08.220
but let's try x is
equal to negative three.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:11.650
So you could try to evaluate
p of negative three.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.140
You could just evaluate that.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.510
Actually, let's just do that.

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:19.350
So that's going to be equal
to negative one times,

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.550
two times negative three is negative six,

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.350
minus three is negative nine,

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.510
negative nine times
negative three minus four,

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:28.693
that is negative seven.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.540
So if you were to
multiply all of this out,

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:36.540
this would give you negative
63, which is clearly negative.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:39.616
So over this interval right over here,

00:05:39.616 --> 00:05:43.060
our polynomial is going to be negative.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.410
So then we can move on to the next one.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:46.580
An interesting thing
is we didn't even have

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:47.870
to figure out the 63 part.

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:49.860
We can just see that there's
a negative times a negative

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:52.620
times a negative, which
is going to be a negative.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.300
And so let's just do that going forward.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:55.950
Let's just think about whether each

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.650
of these are going to
be positive or negative

00:05:57.650 --> 00:05:58.830
and what would happen when you multiply

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:01.100
those positive and negatives together.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:05.900
Now, in this second interval
between negative two and 3/2,

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.360
what is going to happen?

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.010
Well, we could do a sample point.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:12.270
Let's say x is equal to zero.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:14.210
That might be pretty straightforward.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:16.210
Well, when x is equal to zero,

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:19.040
we're going to be dealing with a positive

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:21.920
times a negative times a negative,

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.380
a positive times a
negative times a negative.

00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:25.620
And the reason why I
did that is in my head.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:28.540
I said, okay, that's
going to be a positive two

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:31.090
times a negative three
times a negative four.

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.860
So a positive times a
negative times a negative,

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:34.730
so I could write it this way.

00:06:34.730 --> 00:06:37.970
It's going to be a
positive times a negative

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:39.210
times a negative.

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.720
Well, a negative times a
negative is a positive,

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:43.980
and a positive times a
positive is a positive.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.130
So we are positive over that interval.

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.210
And if you were to evaluate p of zero,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.900
you will get a positive value.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:52.880
Now what about this next interval?

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.490
What about this next interval
here between 3/2 and four?

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:59.400
We could try x is equal to two.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.440
At when x is equal to two,

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.230
we are going to get a positive

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:07.640
times a positive

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:13.350
times a, two minus four is
negative, times a negative.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:16.960
So this is going to be
negative over that interval.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.850
And then last but not least,
when x is greater than four,

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:26.160
we could try x is equal to let's say five.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:29.230
We are going to have a positive,

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:32.710
positive times a positive

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.520
times a positive.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.670
So we are going to have a positive.

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:40.720
And as I mentioned,

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:42.670
you could also do it
without the sample points.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.230
You could say, okay, when
x is greater than four,

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:47.030
you could say, okay, for
any x greater than four,

00:07:47.030 --> 00:07:50.110
if you add two to it, that for
sure is going to be positive.

00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:52.340
For any x greater than four,

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.420
if you multiply it by
two and subtract three,

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.730
well, that's still going to be positive

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:59.380
'cause two times something
greater than four

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:00.800
is definitely greater than three.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:02.430
And for any x greater than four,

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:03.530
if you subtract four from it,

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:05.430
you're still going to
have a positive value.

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:06.750
So that's another way to think about it,

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:10.030
even if you don't use a sample point.

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.500
But there you have it, we
figured out the intervals

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:16.330
over which the function
is negative or positive.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.020
And we don't know exactly
what the function looks like,

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:20.610
but generally speaking it's negative

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:21.530
over this first interval.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:22.730
So it might look something like this.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.780
It's positive over that next interval.

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:28.460
And then it's negative
over that third interval.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:30.980
And then it's positive
over that last interval.

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.550
So we'd have a general shape like this.

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.610
We don't know, without
trying out more points,

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:38.463
exactly how high or low
it would actually go.

